Rendering should only *describe* the UI. Things that reach **outside** React (the tab title, timers, fetching data) are **side effects**. Put them in `useEffect`:

```
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Gold: ${gold}`;
}, [gold]);   // run again whenever gold changes
```

The array at the end lists the **dependencies**.

Svelte has **blocks** for logic in markup:

```
{#each torches as torch (torch.id)}
  <li>{torch.name}</li>
{/each}

{#if count === 0}
  <p>Darkness...</p>
{:else}
  <p>{count} lit</p>
{/if}
```

`(torch.id)` is the key. With `$state`, arrays and objects are deeply reactive: `torch.lit = !torch.lit` just works.

